# What must never go into a public repository.
#
# The first block is the important one. "Sample clips" is 150 MB of real
# newspaper clippings - other people's copyrighted photographs, gathered for an
# internal department report. It is the material the program was built against
# and it must not be published. It is also read-only working material: nothing
# in this project writes to it.
/Sample clips/
/extracted/
/extracted_pdf/

# Built things. The zip that people download belongs on a GitHub Release, not
# in the repository - a release asset can be 2 GB, a file committed to the
# repository cannot go past 100 MB, and the build is 97 MB.
/dist/
/build/
*.spec.bak

# The virtual environment. Hundreds of megabytes of other people's packages,
# rebuildable from requirements in a minute.
/.venv/
/env/
/venv/

# Python leavings
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Development screenshots and page renders left in the root. There are about
# thirty of these, and it is not the disk space that matters - several of them
# are pictures of the program with a real morning's clippings on screen, so
# they carry other people's newspaper photographs into a PUBLIC repository the
# same way the Sample clips folder would. Anything genuinely wanted as
# documentation can be committed deliberately with "git add -f".
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.pdf
/*.docx

# Scratch work and anything a run leaves behind. Anchored for the same reason
# the settings rules above are: bare "prototypes/" would drop a package
# directory of that name at any depth, and this project already lost a shipped
# config file once to an unanchored rule.
/prototypes/
*.log
*.tmp
~$*.docx
~$*.xlsx

# Settings belong to whoever is running it, never to the repository. Everything
# the program remembers lives in %APPDATA%\ClippingsManager, so nothing here
# should ever appear - this is a backstop in case one is copied in to look at.
#
# EVERY RULE HERE IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, and that is not decoration.
# Written bare, "categories.json" matches a file of that name at ANY depth - and
# clippings_manager/config/categories.json is SHIPPED source, the 62 publications
# the program is born knowing. Unanchored, this block silently deleted it from
# the repository, and the first person to clone the project got a build that
# could not start. The settings copy only ever appears at the root, so the root
# is the only place worth guarding.
/categories.json
/sections.json
/wordlist.json
/duplicate_training.jsonl
/duplicate_verdicts.jsonl
/ClippingsManager-setup*.json

# Agent and editor working state, not project source.
.claude/

# Windows and editors
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/
